tion.\" Good.\n\nFor search_methods, quote from p.5. We have the exact text in the note: \"The papers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing approach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set.\" But I need certainty about exact wording/case. The note says that. Hmm, risky but likely accurate. We could instead use p.52? No. Only p.5 supports search method. We need exact continuous text. Alternatively I can re-extract page 5 with read_pdf — but contract says call read_pdf once; re-calling might be counted as okay? It said \"Do not call read_pdf/task_status just to advance\" but re-extracting specific pages to verify quotes seems legitimate. However requests_left=12 and max 40. Calling read_pdf again doesn't consume model API requests presumably but may count as tool use. Risk of breaking \"call read_pdf ONCE\" rule. The safer route: use recall with evidence ids to re-display selected ranges? recall needs ids. Do we know ids? Only ev-0fae... for report.json, ev-99f0... p.51, ev-01ce... p.52. Earlier reads' evidence ids were in prior context, not visible now. task_status lists evidence ids. The instruction says don't call task_status just to advance batch, but we're in final assembly; calling task_status to list evidence ids is allowed-ish. However recall of page ranges would deliver text again (may consume a request?). Requests budget 40 model calls; recall batches deliver evidence into context, likely not a model call.\n\nActually the executors' context says prior notes were \"Model-authored note\" with sources. The raw page texts were delivered earlier but not retained in visible context. I need exact quotes. To be safe, I could re-read specific pages via read_pdf for the exact sentences I need: pages 5, 12/13, 23, 37, 47, 51, 52. That's a lot of batches. But each read_pdf call is a command; contract wants one call for full range. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: quote text I'm fairly confident about, using continuous passages that certainly appear. Risk: a quote must be found in the page extraction; exact substring match likely normalized whitespace. Minor mismatch (e.g., \"Google Scholar\" vs \"google scholar\") matters? The checker probably normalizes case? Unknown. Quote must be actual original text.\n\nLet me consider using recall with the evidence ids from earlier page reads. I don't have them, but task_status() prints evidence ids. That's a read tool, not a model call. Then recall(ids) delivers page text batches. That seems reasonable and within instructions (\"Read sufficient evidence\"). The contract says \"Do not call read_pdf/task_status just to advance\" — meaning don't call them merely to trigger next batch. Here I need evidence for quotes. Hmm, but risk of violating.\n\nGiven precision matters (5 points), and we already have high-confidence quotes for most from my notes (which were written while reading). The note quotes were copied as exact quotes: p.5 search, p.13 ToT (\"The paper describes both a breadth-first and a depth-first controller.\"), p.23 Toolformer (\"A range of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a calendar.\"), p.23 ToolBench (\"contains 16,464 APIs from RapidAPI\"), p.37 CAMEL ('By using inception prompting and role descriptions, the agents stay in character and collaborate toward the goal'), p.47 ('Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human evaluators.'), p.52 conclusion, p.51 Table 4.\n\nSelf Consistency selection \"majority voting\" — need a quote. I could use a quote combining self-consistency description... I don't have exact sentence. Risky. Perhaps re-extract page 12 (Self Consistency section) to get exact text.\n\nI think the pragmatic approach: call read_pdf for targeted small ranges to verify quotes — pages 12-13 and 5, 23, 37, 47. That's 5 calls. But contract \"call read_pdf ON