reflection', 'Safety', 'self-verification', 'benefit'])\n```", "code_action": "def show(res, kws, width=260):\n    for p in res['pages']:\n        for line in p['text'].split('\\n'):\n            if any(k.lower() in line.lower() for k in kws):\n                print(p['page'], '|', line.strip()[:width])\n\nshow(read_pdf(1,2), ['reason', 'act,', 'interact', 'three', 'categories'])\nprint('---')\nshow(read_pdf(12,13), ['majority', 'breadth-first', 'consistent'])\nprint('---')\nshow(read_pdf(23,23), ['calculator', '16,464'])\nprint('---')\nshow(read_pdf(37,37), ['inception'])\nprint('---')\nshow(read_pdf(46,47), ['inception prompting', '100 human'])\nprint('---')\nshow(read_pdf(51,52), ['Training Data', 'Hallucination', 'Agent Behavior', 'Self reflection', 'Safety', 'self-verification', 'benefit'])", "observations": "Execution logs:\n1 | Methods: Agentic LLMs are LLMs that (1) reason, (2) act, and (3) interact. We organize the literature according to these three\n1 | categories.\n1 | Results: The research in the first category focuses on reasoning, reflection, and retrieval, aiming to improve decision making;\n1 | category focuses on multi-agent systems, aiming for collaborative task solving and simulating interaction to study emergent\n1 | social behavior. We find that works mutually benefit from results in other categories: retrieval enables tool use, reflection\n1 | improves multi-agent collaboration, and reasoning benefits all categories.\n1 | in medical diagnosis, logistics and financial market analysis. Meanwhile, self-reflective agents playing roles and interacting\n1 | 1.1      Agentic LLMs: Reasoning–Acting–Interacting                                                                                     3\n2 | 2       Reasoning                                                                                                 8\n2 | 2.1        Multi Step Reasoning                                                                                   9\n2 | 4       Interacting                                                                                              31\n2 | 4.2        Role-Based Interaction                                                                                35\n2 | abilities with reasoning, information retrieval, and interaction tools. As a result, LLMs are now increasingly able\n2 | areas. Their ability to self-reflect, interact, and play roles enables new types of research, including large-scale\n2 | language models that (1) reason, (2) act, and (3) interact. We organize this article accordingly.\n2 | further, and training performance is reportedly plateauing, at high energy cost [Sutskever, 2024]. By interacting\n---\n13 | has been traversed, the search backtracks, and tries an alternative. The paper describes both a breadth-first and a\n---\n23 | range of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a\n23 | calendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs\n---\n37 | without continuous human prompting. By using inception prompting and role descriptions, the agents stay in\n---\n47 | Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human\n---\n51 | Training Data       Finetune with inference time reasoning data\n51 | Hallucination       Use Self Verification\n51 | Agent Behavior      Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing\n51 | Self reflection     In-model self reflection and metareasoning\n51 | Safety              Assistants: Responsibility, liability\n51 | Safety. Safety is a crucial issue in LLMs that act in the world. The problem is studied, but far from being solved\n51 | Ensuring the safety of agentic LLMs requires moving beyond prompt-based defenses toward integrated, multi-\n51 | layer safeguards. Key directions include embedding explicit safety constraints within the agent’s reasoning\n51 | awareness, enabling agents to detect and avoid unsafe actions, and establishing rigorous, standardized safety\n51 | Clearly, many safety, ethics and trust issues will have to be addressed before the full breadth of the possibilities\n51 | of agentic LLMs can be enjoyed. Safety and ethics will become important topics on the research agenda of agentic\n52 | build on grounded retrieval augmentation, social interaction (such as theory of mind) builds on self reflection,\n52 | and all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification. Closing the cycle, the acting and interacting\n52 | categories generate training data for further pretraining and finetuning LLMs, beyond plateauing traditional\nLast output from code snippet:\nNone", "observations_images": null, "action_output": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 9513, "output_tokens": 1421, "total_tokens": 10934}, "is_final_answer": false}}
