ion/reflection retry.\n\nWrite code now. Careful with quotes windows: I'll collapse whitespace.\n\nImplement:\n\ndef w(page, key, before, after, texts):\n    t = texts.get(page,'')\n    i = t.find(key)\n    if i<0: return None\n    s = t[max(0,i-before): i+len(key)+after]\n    return re.sub(r'\\s+',' ',s).strip()\n\nNeed texts dict for pages 5,7,12,13,23,37,47,51,52 → read_pdf(5,52) would be huge; but read_pdf delivers bounded batches and returns dict with pages. Earlier pgt calls worked with single pages. Calling read_pdf once for 5-52 might deliver only partial pages (queued). Safer: call per page? That's 9 calls but they're cheap. Actually earlier note: read_pdf returns pages list — for large range may truncate. Do per-page calls (allowed; already did full 52 earlier). Each call rc 0.\n\nThen build report, write, pytest.\n\nIf a quote is None (keyword not found), I'll fall back to page 1 abstract? Not good. Instead I'll print diagnostics and still write with best available fallback: use the whole trimmed page text first 500 chars? That would likely not support. I'll just accept; but to improve odds, print the None and page text snippet at end for... no more requests. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively: for robustness on Table 4 (page 51), fetch wider: for agenda_topics, take the page 51 text region containing \"Topic\" and 5 keywords... better: instead of a window, construct a quote by locating \"Training Data\" and \"Safety\" and slicing between them — but window with before=1000 after=200 similar.\n\nLet me just also print all computed quotes so I can see (in output) whether they look right; can't fix but at least know score.\n\nActually — maybe I can do two actions if final_answer counts as the last allowed request (requests_after_response 1). Sequence: action A (this response) = write report + pytest. Action B = final_answer. That's 2 responses; requests used: A uses 1 (39), B uses... final answer maybe not counted. Risky but the best I can do given the unknowns. If A fai